LONG-TERM BUILD RULE — ONE SMALL STEP, THEN MY REVIEW

Build The Broken Supply Route slowly with me, one testable increment per request. At the start of a pass, name the one outcome I will be able to see or verify. Implement that increment and the minimum support it needs. The rest of this brief is the long-term design contract. Its feature lists and Chapter 1 checks become relevant as we reach them. Finishing a step early is the point to hand it back to me.

STEP 1 ONLY FOR A NEW PROJECT — Open the native Android app to the Broken Supply Route dispatch. Show the Guided and Compact options with a short explanation of each. Let me pick one, show which I picked, and persist the preference locally. The check is simple: choose Compact, fully close and relaunch the app, and see that Compact is still selected. The same must work for Guided. Keep this step to that screen and its saved preference. Starter grants, currencies, Hall training, squad setup and combat have their own later steps.

IF THIS PROJECT ALREADY EXISTS — Read BUILD_STATE.md if present and inspect the actual app. Resume at the earliest unfinished agreed step without recreating the project or clearing my save. If I give feedback, address it within the current step. If a check fails, keep that step open and fix or report the blocker.

STOP AFTER EACH STEP — Tell me what changed, how I can check it on Android, which checks you actually ran, and the single next step you recommend. Save that checkpoint in BUILD_STATE.md. Then wait for my feedback or my explicit “continue.” One “continue” authorizes one more step. Combine steps only if I explicitly ask.

Build The Broken Supply Route as a playable, portrait-first native Android game in Google AI Studio’s “Build an Android app” mode. Use a runnable Kotlin/Android SDK project with Jetpack Compose. Keep each increment buildable as a debug APK. This is a private, disposable game-design experiment based on Naruto Idle RPG Master Game Theory & Design Bible v0.10.32 (2 November 2026, through §59). I am the designer. Across successive passes, build toward a playable Chapter 1 with real game state and combat outcomes. The current turn follows the one-step rule above. I want to play a new account, make a squad decision, see its consequence and resume my progress after closing and relaunching the installed app.

01 / The game I want to play

I lead five shinobi escorting medical supplies along a broken route. An injured courier, a convoy ambush and a silenced warning form the first chapter. The interesting decision is who should stand where, which threat the squad should answer, and what it gives up to do so. During my absence the account earns common materials. When I return, I choose how to spend them, who to deploy and whether the next fight needs a different approach. A battle recap should let me explain a win or loss in ordinary language.

Keep the identity of the long-term Naruto-inspired project: small squads, shinobi roles, personal Chakra, techniques, relationships, village affiliations and tactical counters. For this experiment use original characters, locations, insignia, dialogue, audio and artwork. Keep stable content IDs independent of names, art and localization. A future licensed theme must be replaceable without changing saves, combat math or entitlements.

Composition should beat thoughtless power stacking when the matchup favors it. Give every important threat two accessible response categories. A player should be able to field the first copy of an owned counter at the squad's earned baseline. Favor optional specialization, sidegrades, cosmetics and time savings. Solo campaign progression must work without a purchase, lucky draw, duplicate threshold, paid gear, perfect attendance, guild or PvP rank.

